inflater: resolve a numeric string reference in android:text / android:hint

LayoutInflater.resolveString handled `@string/name` and `@android:string/name`
and returned anything else verbatim. A layout whose string entry carries no
name reaches it as `@0x7f1379b6`, and that literal became the text: the search
field of Instagram's share sheet showed "@0x7f1379b6" as its hint. Resolve it
through the context's Resources (the app's subclass when it serves its own
strings), the rule TypedArray.getString already applies, and never show the
literal. After: the hint reads "Pesquisar".

diff --git a/runtime/java/android/view/LayoutInflater.java b/runtime/java/android/view/LayoutInflater.java
--- a/runtime/java/android/view/LayoutInflater.java
+++ b/runtime/java/android/view/LayoutInflater.java
@@ -5790,6 +5790,22 @@
 
     private String resolveString(String s) {
         if (s == null || s.isEmpty()) return s;
+        // A numeric reference (`@0x7f1379b6`, how a packed layout names a
+        // string whose entry carries no name) is a reference all the same:
+        // resolve it through the context's Resources -- the APP's subclass
+        // when it serves its own strings -- and never show the literal. This
+        // is the same rule TypedArray.getString applies.
+        if (s.length() > 3 && s.charAt(0) == '@' && s.charAt(1) == '0'
+                && (s.charAt(2) == 'x' || s.charAt(2) == 'X')) {
+            try {
+                int id = (int) Long.parseLong(s.substring(3), 16);
+                if (id != 0 && context != null && context.getResources() != null) {
+                    CharSequence resolved = context.getResources().getText(id);
+                    if (resolved != null) return resolved.toString();
+                }
+            } catch (Throwable ignored) {}
+            return null;
+        }
         if (s.startsWith("@string/")) {
